**1. `build_decompress_attrmap` off-by-one / name-matching bug (compression.c, new function around line ~1343-1405):**

```c
for (i = 0; i < outnatts; i++)
{
    Form_pg_attribute outatt = TupleDescAttr(compressed_desc, i);
    ...
    if (outatt->attisdropped)
        continue;

    attname = NameStr(outatt->attname);

    if (strcmp(attname, COMPRESSION_COLUMN_METADATA_COUNT_NAME) == 0)
    {
        *count_meta_attnum = outatt->attnum;
        continue;
    }

    for (j = 0; j < innatts; j++)
    {
        Form_pg_attribute inatt;
        nextindesc++;
        if (nextindesc >= innatts)
            nextindesc = 0;
        inatt = TupleDescAttr(noncompressed_desc, nextindesc);
        if (inatt->attisdropped)
            continue;
        if (strcmp(attname, NameStr(inatt->attname)) == 0)
        {
            attrMap->attnums[i] = inatt->attnum;
            break;
        }
    }
}
```

This inner loop is really suspicious. `nextindesc` is a shared state variable across the outer loop iterations. It starts at -1. For each outer attribute, it does a linear scan starting from `nextindesc+1` (modulo innatts), looking for a name match. The loop aborts on the first match only — but if after finding the (wrong) column it scanned past, no. Actually there's a serious logic problem: the inner loop only ever considers one "pass" through — if `nextindesc` wraps around, it won't scan all innatts. Actually, `nextindesc++` then check `>= innatts` resets to 0. So within one inner loop, it increments up to innatts times before the loop terminates (j < innatts). But since the reset happens, it can scan at most innatts positions, but with wrapping, some columns may be checked twice and others not at all.

More importantly: because `nextindesc` is persistent across outer iterations, the scan doesn't restart from the beginning for each output column. This is a heuristic meant to match in order assuming same-order columns, but it's a fragile/incorrect algorithm: if the naming convention doesn't align with order, it can fail to match a column that exists, or match the wrong one. And crucially, there's no error handling for a column that fails to match — `attrMap->attnums[i]` just stays default. Actually `make_attrmap` initializes attnums... let me recall. make_attrmap creates with all zeros typically. So unmatched → attnum 0 → InvalidAttrNumber. In `create_per_compressed_column`, `decompressed_colnum = decompressor->attrmap->attnums[col]`, and if not valid → treated as a column with no uncompressed version. So the failure mode is silent: a genuinely-matching column could be dropped, producing wrong decompressed output (data loss/incorrect data) without error.

Also `count_meta_attnum` is set but the function asserts it's valid after. The header's `get_attnum` semantics: original code used `get_attnum` by name lookup. This new hand-rolled windowed match changes correctness. This is a real concern — wrong-results/data-corruption risk. And compared to the original `get_attnum(out_rel->rd_id, col_name)` it's strictly worse.

Let me note the `Assert(AttributeNumberIsValid(count_meta_attnum));` — the previous code asserted failure loudly, now this is an Assert (only in debug builds). But count metadata column always present, so fine.

